SM(586,{"n":"Philemon","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Philemon.mp3","d":2774.8,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.64,"Now we'll talk about the even shorter epistle of St."],[30.08,"Paul to uh St."],[31.6,"Philemon. Um this is actually this epistle was actually written much earlier."],[38.08,"We've talked before about how St."],[40.96,"Paul's epistles are organized in order of length, from biggest to smallest, not in chronological order."],[49.12,"Um and with some exceptions, like they put first and second Corinthians together in 1st and second Thessalonians and 1st and 2nd Timothy, but in general, they're from longest or short."],[59.92,"So uh the epistle to St"],[61.88,"Philemon is last only because uh it's the shortest."],[68.68,"Um we'll get into Hebrews next week when we start talking about about Hebrews, but the earliest copies of Hebrews that the earliest copy of Hebrews that we have is actually part of a collection of St."],[82.28,"Paul's epistles, and it's not at the end, it's right after Romans, because length."],[88.49,"Um it's been moved uh in our modern English New Testaments based on people's uh belief that St."],[98.49,"Paul didn't write it."],[99.93,"So it was pulled out of St."],[101.21,"Paul's epistles. Uh this I'll only comment on this. briefly here, but maybe more next time when we start talking about Hebrews"],[108.13,"Um we kind of take for granted the order of the books in the New Testament."],[113.73,"Uh this isn't what they always were."],[118.31,"In in the fourth century, uh most of the Greek uh Bibles and New Testaments we have uh didn't have the book of Revelation at all."],[129.19,"And they uh had they went Gospels, general epistles, James, 1 and second Peter, first  sec2ndond, 3rd John, those books, then Saint Paul's epistles."],[143.99,"Uh so they're in a different order than what we have."],[146.55,"So th this order's fairly recent, and, the and order the. order of books of books of the Old Testament is all over the place"],[152.83,"You may have noticed if you've looked through an Orthodox Study Bible that they're in a totally different order."],[162.51,"Even though our Protestant friends accept the Hebrew Bible's canon as their canon of the Old Testament, they don't have the books in the same order as they are in the Hebrew Bible, which is different than the order of the Greek Old Testament, which is different than the Slavonic order."],[181.88,"So the order of books, uh, I'll just say this."],[187.4,"If you ever hear anyone trying to make any kind of theological argument based on the order of the books in the Bible, you should probably ignore everything they say."],[201.04,"Because that's kind of a sure sign that they don't know a lot about the Bible and its history."],[210.0,"Because yeah, it's it's it's changed a lot."],[213.12,"But anyway, so the point being, St."],[215.68,"Paul's epistle to St."],[216.56,"Philemon. That's why we get to this very short epistle uh here at the end."],[220.96,"Um it was written at a different time, though."],[223.52,"It was written around the same time as Ephesians and Colossians."],[227.84,"So this would have been when Saint Paul is in prison in Ephesus, as we talked about."],[234.0,"Um he was in prison a lot."],[237.2,"This is a much earlier. period"],[239.16,"So we're probably talking about around 50 or 51 AD, as opposed to the mid 60s for the pastoral epistles that we just read."],[249.64,"Um what this shares in common with them, though, of course, is that it's written to a single person, not to a community."],[259.64,"That said, it's written to a single person as a public letter."],[265.32,"So um it's written to a particular person dealing with a particular issue."],[273.4,"Um as a public letter."],[278.57,"So this would be the equivalent of uh if I had some kind of, I'm gonna pick on Arthur."],[284.93,"If I had some kind of issue with Arthur's behavior, and I sent him a letter saying, Oh, my good friend Arthur, I'm not gonna tell you what to do."],[297.25,"I'm not gonna come down hard on you."],[299.89,"Uh, but I really think you should rethink XYZ."],[304.61,"And I really think this is what you should do."],[308.21,"And then I made 50 copies of it and gave them to everyone in church."],[314.7,"So the way in which it was put out kind of belies the tone I'm taking."],[322.16,"There would be a lot more onus, on the person to sort of follow through on what was said in it based on everyone looking."],[331.95,"Um so uh you have to kind of keep that in mind as we read it, that St."],[339.15,"Paul is being very soft and very deferent to St."],[346.27,"Philemon about this, but he's also speaking with authority by nature of how this is all happening."],[356.72,"Um the issue is that he's addressing is uh that um Saint Onesimus, who is also a saint, uh, was an enslaved person."],[369.04,"int. Sa Philemon was his master"],[376.6,"Both of them are Christians."],[379.88,"And Saint Onesimus, there was some kind of issue that caused him to flee."],[390.66,"Which, as an enslaved person, obviously you're not allowed to do."],[394.44,"That's so one of the first rules."],[396.6,"Uh so he came to Saint Paul to ask Saint Paul to intercede for him with Saint Philemon."],[409.16,"Uh this was a not. uncommon occurrence in the ancient world"],[417.84,"If you were an enslaved person who is uh had been somehow disobedient, or there had been a misunderstanding, or you'd actually stolen something or done something right, or had just fled, or whatever, a very common way to try to get back in good graces and get out of trouble."],[438.37,"Uh would be to go to someone who had a person of higher social status than the person to whom you were enslaved and asked them to intervene."],[451.97,"So we have, for example, uh one of the letters of Pliny that we have is Pliny doing exactly what St."],[457.41,"Paul is doing here"],[464.09,"Basically writing a letter on behalf of this enslaved person back to his master to try to put the situation in order."],[474.41,"Pliny adopts a somewhat different tone."],[483.69,"But so this was this was a way of doing that."],[486.74,"If if you were a runaway slave, or you were a slave who was it had some other problem with your master, the the means you had to try to correct that would be to go to somebody who was of higher social status."],[500.66,"So because the parties involved are Christians, St."],[501.78,"the Paul is an apostle has higher social status."],[508.94,"So he wants uh him to correct this."],[512.7,"Um I'll talk a little bit more, since I said I would, about St."],[517.18,"Paul's view of slavery."],[519.18,"Um St. Paul does not consider slavery a good thing."],[524.06,"Okay. So for example, we don't have to go that far back."],[529.1,"In 1 Timothy, chapter 1, he gave a list of of uh uh bad folks."],[540.75,"Um in chapter 1, verses 9 and ten."],[547.69,"He lists all these bad folks the unholy, the profane, murderers of fathers, murderers of mothers, manslayers, fornicators, sodomites."],[556.91,"And here in the New King James it says kidnappers."],[563.47,"And uh what it actually says in Greek is anthropodestes."],[567.39,"That literally means Caesars of men."],[569.87,"That's talking about slave traders."],[572.99,"That's slave traders."],[574.11,"That's people who enslave other people and then sell them."],[576.91,"Okay. So St."],[578.11,"Paul lists them alongside people who murder their parents."],[582.27,"So he does not consider slavery to be this great thing."],[586.43,"Okay. Um that said. we have to talk about slavery in the Roman world"],[594.63,"Because there are continuities and discontinuities, shall we say, between what we think of, especially as modern Americans."],[607.51,"When we think of slavery, we're thinking of the North Atlantic slave trade."],[612.31,"We're thinking of chattel slavery, the enslavement of Africans, but also of natives, more than we're aware."],[622.95,"And forced labor that they were subjected to chattel slavery in the United States."],[628.13,"So there are obvious clear points of continuity."],[634.18,"In both. cases, these are humans who are owned by another human"],[639.74,"These are humans who are, despite being humans, considered subhuman."],[644.88,"You don't have the racial element in Roman slavery."],[648.61,"Um but uh, and I won't digress too much on this, but you actually, at the beginning of the North Atlantic slave trade, didn't have a racial element really in that racism grew out of slavery to justify it rather than the other way around."],[664.61,"Um but uh these are people who are owned, uh, these are people who are being exploited in every way."],[676.53,"Uh and I'll try to do this as as delicately possible."],[680.73,"But meaning this isn't just a question of this is our labor force, our free labor force who will literally work to death if we want to, which is true."],[692.73,"But also sexual exploitation of slaves was common in both cases."],[701.53,"And we saw, I hope I talked about it delicately enough, but not so delicately that people didn't know what we were talking about."],[708.89,"But we saw that St."],[709.93,"Paul even said to enslaved people at one point talked, literally talked to them in one of his epistles about being raped."],[720.13,"Because that was a reality for an enslaved person in the Roman world, just like in child slavery that took place in the Americas, that they could be used sexually at any time."],[733.09,"They had no say in it."],[734.55,"And so how St."],[736.07,"Paul talked about how talked to them about how when that was done to them, they were not sinning in any way."],[745.51,"They were not being sexually immoral, no matter what their body did when it happened."],[750.07,"They were not sexually immoral, they were not wicked, they were not participating."],[755.16,"And extended the hope of the gospel to them and God's healing to them."],[760.25,"But that was that was a reality."],[762.81,"Okay. Um what you find. not only in St"],[772.37,"Paul, but this is drawn out by the church fathers."],[774.93,"Because of course, Roman slavery didn't suddenly go away."],[778.45,"It was still around in St."],[779.49,"John Chrysostom's time."],[780.93,"We find St."],[781.57,"Gregory of Nyssa preaching against it."],[784.85,"Two Paschal homilies in a row."],[788.21,"He said to the the people in his church, you can't own your fellow Christians."],[795.91,"You need to free them."],[797.21,"This is incompatible with the gospel."],[799.79,"We see how humanity, what humanity is and how humanity has been transformed in Christ."],[804.43,"This is incompatible."],[810.95,"And so this element of it, uh this exploitative element of it, and especially the sexually exploitative element of it, is attacked directly by Christianity and by Christians."],[824.15,"Say John Chrysostom actually goes on and on about it."],[826.79,"It's not stuff people read."],[829.51,"But it it was still a problem, even apparently for his Christian congregants, that he had to rail against them sexually exploiting enslaved people in their households."],[844.47,"Yeah, exactly. Um so he he had to, but he did, he did rail against it."],[853.48,"Now the other side, the side of discontinuity, is that the institution of slavery in the Roman Empire served a different function and purpose and had a slightly different structure."],[868.13,"In American slavery."],[871.01,"Slavery was used, for example, in the Roman Empire at times."],[876.77,"Not everyone who was enslaved as this way."],[878.85,"There were people who were enslaved because they lost a war."],[881.31,"Lost a battle, got annexed by Rome, whatever."],[884.75,"But many of the people, it was a debt issue."],[889.05,"Where you had contracted a debt that you couldn't pay, they didn't have a bankruptcy system, and so you would essentially work off the debt for the person to whom you were indebted."],[898.81,"debt It's sort of like collection agencies today, they could sell that and you to someone else."],[905.11,"Um so it served that function."],[909.83,"But also, also, uh, enslaved people were part, not a very high-ranking part, but were part of Roman households under the Potter Familius."],[924.71,"And this is true earlier in the ancient world as well."],[928.23,"So there's there are provisions in the Torah, for example, for someone who has been enslaved."],[932.97,"They have to be freed after a certain amount of time."],[935.62,"In the Jubilee year, they all get freed."],[938.04,"In in the Torah, if it were followed, which we know Israel didn't do."],[941.88,"But there's also provision there for if someone at the time when they're to be free wants to remain part of the household."],[953.94,"Wants to remain part of the household."],[955.7,"That they can stay if that's what they want."],[958.66,"And the ritual there, this is a rabbit hole, but we'll talk about it more when we get to Exodus, when we loop back around after Revelation to get to Exodus."],[966.34,"But the ritual there is you actually take them to the entrance to the home, and they are ritually brought before the ancestors, before the previous generations of the family, and incorporated into the household permanently through that ritual."],[981.62,"They're permanently made part of the family."],[985.0,"So there were enslaved persons who were, for example, they didn't have public schools."],[991.78,"So if you were a wealthy family, you would have an enslaved person who taught your children, who educated your children."],[1000.18,"And they might be well treated and like that enragement."],[1005.86,"So there were a certain number of people."],[1016.34,"Technically, he's Batman's servant."],[1018.91,"He's Bruce Wayne's servant."],[1020.43,"He's a manservant there."],[1021.95,"But he's really part of the family."],[1023.87,"He's almost his surrogate dad."],[1025.89,"I mean, he's like."],[1027.23,"But there were people who served in these different roles and families and developed these relationships."],[1029.71,"So it wasn't always completely exploitative"],[1036.35,"And so St."],[1038.35,"Paul allows for that."],[1039.63,"And so for some of these people, especially people who had that kind of role, but even some people who didn't, if they were just suddenly freed, tossed out of the household."],[1054.59,"It's not like they could just go get a job."],[1056.92,"We're pre capitalism by a lot."],[1059.18,"They didn't necessarily know a trade that they could go out and earn a living at."],[1065.82,"There were guilds of freedmen that tried to do that"],[1069.76,"Right? So when somebody like earned their freedom and they were out there, oh, it's sort of like, oh, what do I do now?"],[1075.12,"Sort of like people getting out of prison today."],[1077.2,"You know, what what did you support myself?"],[1080.64,"There were guilds, sort of trade guilds of freedmen who were other people who had sort of worked their way out of slavery or been granted their freedom, who would then they would join those guilds to help learn trades and support each other and that kind of thing in the Roman world."],[1097.84,"So um, and and so there were reasons why people might not want to just have the institution done away with."],[1107.18,"Until society were restructured in a different kind of way."],[1112.54,"And so this is why you don't find St."],[1116.22,"Paul or even most of the church fathers."],[1116.62,"or But um, St."],[1118.66,"St. Gregory just says you have to free everybody."],[1123.14,"John Chrysostom. St."],[1124.28,"John Chrysostom is going to set these very strict rules for how you have to treat and relate to, and that, especially if they're a Christian, but even if they're not, uh, an enslaved person who's part of your household is part of your household."],[1139.24,"And the respect and dignity that they and their body needs to be treated with."],[1144.01,"But he doesn't come out and say, you have to free them, no matter what the circumstances are, whether they want it or not."],[1152.75,"You have to remove them from your household."],[1155.31,"And so, for a s for certain modern readers, nothing is acceptable from the authors of scripture or from the church fathers, except the institution of slavery is evil and must end now today."],[1174.92,"But that's not how the real world works."],[1180.73,"You can't, Rome wasn't built in a day, you can't restructure Rome in a day."],[1186.17,"And all of Roman life and all of the social institutions in a day."],[1189.45,"Some of them have to go immediately."],[1191.55,"So Christians were clear."],[1192.67,"Pederasty has to go immediately."],[1194.67,"That is a social institution with zero value, it's done."],[1198.72,"Certain elements of slavery have to go immediately."],[1202.24,"But there's other elements that can be worked out over time"],[1207.06,"That are not initially demeaning and degrading."],[1210.18,"Yes. Aren't there at this time lots of craftsmen and people with small shops and stuff who are slaves?"],[1218.34,"The shop and business really belongs to another."],[1222.26,"I know that was true."],[1222.98,"In some cases, yes."],[1224.1,"I know that was true somewhat earlier, but it's asked to be true."],[1226.34,"Yes, in some cases, yes, because you'd have, you know, you might have a reason on your estate to want to have an enslaved person who is good at a certain trade."],[1240.04,"And then your particular need for that might end."],[1245.98,"You no longer need a blacksmith on your estate per se."],[1246.38,"There's not enough work, for them to do"],[1251.64,"And so that was a way."],[1253.76,"Well, hey, I can make money this way by effectively loading them out to the public."],[1262.02,"And then taking the the proceeds."],[1265.48,"So yeah, that happened."],[1267.16,"That happened. That they were sort of made available publicly that way."],[1272.44,"Or just sold to someone who did need that form of that form of labor."],[1277.48,"Well, but I'm familiar with this in the late republic when they're enslaving thousands of Greeks and things like that."],[1284.2,"Yeah. Some of them are blacksmiths, and so they sent them to the blacksmith shop."],[1288.36,"Yeah, yeah. Yeah."],[1289.16,"ah Ye. But then as you get into generational slavery"],[1290.0,"If they happened to have it."],[1295.82,"Someone who grows up and lives as a slave."],[1297.98,"They were past that really."],[1299.42,"Yeah. Yeah. Um so there would still be some maybe taught by their father or taught by right, so that did still happen."],[1307.42,"But there were also people who were just slaves their whole life."],[1310.76,"And had just done field work."],[1312.76,"I mean remember, this is still primarily an agricultural economy."],[1316.17,"So most of the work is field work."],[1318.65,"That's all they've ever done."],[1320.81,"Uh you're just set free tomorrow."],[1325.85,"What are you gonna do for field work?"],[1327.85,"Go enslave yourself to somebody else."],[1329.69,"I mean, like, it's not like there's you don't own any land yourself, you know, at the end."],[1334.93,"So um so someone would go into the military, which at the end of your Roman military service, you'd get land."],[1341.49,"Umself to somebody else, but for a period of time and then getting some reward at the end."],[1350.21,"Um yeah. Yeah, and in the old testament, you also find that kind of slavery."],[1359.73,"Um where I mean, even uh Jacob does that."],[1364.87,"When he wants to marry Rachel, goes to his uncle Laban and says, I'll work for you for seven years."],[1370.15,"He's out there working in the fields for seven years."],[1372.97,"Because he doesn't have a dowry."],[1375.53,"So he says, That'll be my dowry, I'll work for you seven years."],[1377.37,"And then oh wait, wrong wife"],[1384.29,"Okay, I'll go work another seven years."],[1388.21,"Um yeah, but so that it worked that way too, where you would could make an agreement like that too."],[1394.37,"That's like an indentured servitude."],[1396.37,"Yeah, yeah, yeah."],[1398.05,"The lines between enslavement and indentured servitude were thin."],[1401.89,"The main difference is that enslavement usually involved whole families."],[1409.89,"Whereas indentured servitude was usually an individual for a set period of time. in return for something"],[1418.69,"So, yeah, so um, yeah, and this is. yeah, I'm gonna go into this, I don't care"],[1426.89,"Um, due to the way in which a particular type of Puritan thinking is the basis of American thought, we have a tendency in a very Protestant and Puritan way to think of morality as holding the correct opinion about what is wrong."],[1452.75,"What makes us moral people is not so much what we do as what we believe."],[1457.47,"You can see how this is very Protestant and very Puritan."],[1461.25,"So, what's important is that we all agree that slavery is wrong."],[1466.29,"That's what makes you a moral person"],[1469.85,"And so if someone doesn't come out and say in the ancient world, if St."],[1474.61,"Paul doesn't come out and say, or St."],[1475.81,"John Chrysostom doesn't come out and say, slavery is evil and must be abolished, he's an immoral person because he doesn't have the right opinion about morality."],[1486.05,"And we do this about everything."],[1488.29,"And it doesn't matter where you are on the political spectrum."],[1492.43,"So there are a whole lot of folks, uh I'll get myself canceled, I don't care."],[1499.15,"There are a whole bunch of folks, for example, who think it's really important that we all think abortion is wrong."],[1505.79,"We haven't done anything about abortion."],[1507.15,"about abortion. We haven't tried to help any unwed mothers who are thinking having an to encourage them not to"],[1515.59,"Haven't spent a penny."],[1518.07,"But we feel really good about ourselves that we're good people, moral people, because we all agree it's bad."],[1525.35,"And what makes you bad is not so much going and having an abortion or performing an abortion, it's just not agreeing with us that it's bad."],[1534.31,"That makes you a bad person."],[1535.83,"Because it's about what you believe, not what you do."],[1538.87,"And then just to cover all the ground, this is what all the woke stuff is about."],[1544.39,"We're good people because we think the right things are bad."],[1548.39,"Racism is bad."],[1549.67,"We all agree that's what makes us good people."],[1551.51,"We haven't done anything to help anyone"],[1551.59,"We haven't done anything to help any people."],[1553.59,"one We certainly haven't d anything to help any poor people."],[1554.31,"We don't like them much"],[1559.41,"But we agree that poverty is bad and that makes us good people."],[1563.94,"And so that skews everything."],[1566.26,"Now we read these."],[1567.46,"That's not how St."],[1569.14,"Paul or any of the church fathers approached morality."],[1573.4,"As we just saw in St."],[1574.68,"Paul's Epistle to St."],[1575.8,"Titus. He didn't care what you thought was bad."],[1580.61,"And what you thought was good."],[1582.69,"He cared about the fruit."],[1584.37,"In fact, the problem with those false teachers."],[1587.87,"He didn't say their doctrines are wrong."],[1591.31,"They think things are bad that are really good and things are good that are really bad."],[1596.03,"He said, look you at look at their deeds, you what they do and they're evil."],[1602.01,"What did Christ always say to the Pharisees?"],[1604.49,"The Pharisees thought the right things were good and the right things were bad."],[1610.57,"But he called them hypocrites over and over again because of what they did and how they lived."],[1616.43,"And so St."],[1618.19,"Paul couldn't care less about whether people think slavery is an evil or not."],[1625.95,"What he cares about is how actual enslaved people are treated."],[1630.99,"And how they live their lives as Christians while they're enslaved."],[1636.83,"And them and their masters finding salvation."],[1642.23,"So bad news for hashtag activism."],[1645.93,"Tweeting something, holding an opinion, is not moral or immoral."],[1651.13,"What we do is moral or immoral."],[1654.97,"Christ doesn't say to the goats, you know, you saw that I was in prison and you thought that was really bad."],[1663.37,"And not right."],[1667.85,"He says you either ignored me or you came and you visited me."],[1674.73,"So, yeah, what a brief tangent."],[1676.89,"Sorry. Sometimes I get mad at the nation of my birth."],[1683.37,"Anyway. So. with all that said, we will now read uh St"],[1691.97,"Paul's epistle to St."],[1693.97,"Philemon. I thank my God, making mention of you always in my prayers, hearing of your love and faith which you have toward the Lord Jesus and toward all the saints, that the sharing of your faith may become effective by the acknowledgement of every good thing which is in you in Christ Jesus."],[1713.73,"For we have great joy and consolation in your love, because the hearts of the saints have been refreshed by you, brother."],[1720.45,"Maybe you did it on purpose because you started with verse 4"],[1725.81,"Oh. Thank you."],[1732.81,"So verse I thought that was weird."],[1735.37,"I'm like, where's this usual greeting?"],[1737.29,"Okay. I'm right with you."],[1740.81,"Rewind, start over."],[1742.73,"Uh Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy, our brother, to Philemon, our beloved friend and fellow laborer, to the beloved Ophea Archipus, our fellow soldier, and to the church in your house."],[1762.81,"Paul, prisoner of Christ Jesus."],[1764.49,"He's writing this for prison, and notice he lists Saint Timothy as a co-author, like he did in Ephesians and Colossians from this same period."],[1772.93,"That's one of the ways we know. that it's from that period"],[1776.73,"And he's writing to Philemon, who is his uh the word friend."],[1783.85,"Can't just say he's his beloved."],[1786.57,"Um and uh fellow laborer."],[1790.27,"And then to these these other people who are likely part of his household."],[1795.39,"Uh Apia might be his wife, um, and Archibis might be a son or another member of the household."],[1802.27,"Um, and to the church in your house."],[1804.99,"So his household is sort of the focal point and the meeting place of the local Christian community."],[1813.87,"He's sort of at the hub at the center."],[1815.59,"Do you know where he is?"],[1817.07,"Um I don't think he says."],[1823.99,"Yeah. Both uh St."],[1827.67,"Philemon and St."],[1828.95,"Onesimus uh become bishops later."],[1834.39,"He says Yeah, he's a bishop of Colossae."],[1839.27,"And uh Onesimus became bishop in Ephesus."],[1843.83,"So it's in that, it's somewhere in that region."],[1846.39,"Which would be like which would make sense that St."],[1848.71,"Paul's writing letters to that region."],[1850.55,"And then the Mesilus could go with it to St."],[1852.79,"Paul. Yeah. Yeah."],[1856.15,"If he's imprisoned at Ephesus."],[1858.87,"Um. Grace to you and peace from. God our Father, the Lord Jesus Christ, his usual introduction"],[1867.39,"And then the stuff I read earlier."],[1869.17,"I thank my God, making mention of you always in my prayers, hearing of your love and faith which you have toward the Lord Jesus and toward all the saints, that the sharing of your faith may become effective by the acknowledgement of every good thing which is in you in Christ Jesus."],[1882.37,"For we have great joy and consolation in your love, because the hearts of the saints have been refreshed by you, brother."],[1887.81,"Notice he refers to him as brother."],[1890.37,"This is part of that."],[1891.89,"He's better in your body."],[1893.17,"Yeah. This is part of how."],[1894.67,"And we see this, these are the same kind of things he said when he wrote positive letters to communities."],[1900.13,"These same kind of things."],[1901.17,"I give thanks to you in my prayers because of the good things you're doing."],[1905.5,"And he's he's not lying, I'm sure."],[1907.62,"I mean, say say finally clearly, I mean, especially since he became a bishop later."],[1912.44,"Is clearly an important part of the community, a faithful part of the community."],[1917.64,"Um, and so he's praying that he will continue."],[1922.62,"But yeah, calling him brother rather than son, for example, is right sort of he's sort of coming alongside him and putting his arm around him."],[1931.55,"My good friend, my beloved friend, my brother."],[1935.95,"We need to talk for a second."],[1941.47,"We're saying, therefore, we already got a therefore."],[1946.73,"All this being said, therefore, though I might be very bold in Christ to command you what is fitting, yet for love's sake I rather appeal to you."],[1958.85,"I'm an apostle."],[1959.81,"I mean, I could just tell you what to do."],[1963.25,"But due to our friendship, I will just I will just uh suggest, I will just ask for you to do this."],[1978.77,"Being such a one as Paul the Aged, and now also a prisoner of Christ Jesus."],[1984.77,"Just little old Paul."],[1987.97,"Over here in prison."],[1989.65,"Don't mind me."],[1994.21,"I appeal to you for my son Onesimus, whom I have begotten while in my chains, who once was unprofitable to you, but now is profitable to you and to me."],[2008.5,"So there was this issue."],[2011.35,"I know you thought he was worthless, he was a problem, but you know what?"],[2016.71,"He's come here."],[2018.65,"And now he's like a son to me."],[2021.29,"Spiritual son. And has been very profitable and helpful to me."],[2025.29,"And I'm sure also will be to you."],[2031.53,"Verse 12, I am sending him back."],[2034.65,"You therefore receive him, that is, my own heart, whom I wished to keep with me, that on your behalf he might minister to me in my chains for the gospel."],[2048.53,"I wanted to keep him here to assist me on your behalf, giving you the credit."],[2055.09,"But still I'm sending him back."],[2057.49,"And it's like I'm sending back to you my own heart."],[2063.01,"Remember, St. Paul is publishing this publicly."],[2072.05,"Verse 14, but without your consent, I wanted to do nothing, that your good deed might not be by compulsion, as it were, but voluntary."],[2082.05,"So he's actually offered for doing the right thing here"],[2082.57,"I want you to get full credit."],[2088.41,"The chance to be publicly congratulated for doing it."],[2093.61,"Exactly. To be acknowledged for all the good that he said there in the introduction."],[2099.55,"Now is your chance to show everyone just how beneficent and loving and kind and compassionate you are."],[2109.39,"Verse 15. For perhaps he departed for a while for this purpose, that you might receive him forever."],[2118.05,"Maybe this is why he left in the first place."],[2121.49,"So that he could come to me."],[2123.57,"And so that he could grow in this way."],[2126.05,"And now you can receive him back forever."],[2127.65,"Verse 16 No longer is a slave, but more than a slave, a beloved brother"],[2135.13,"Especially to me, but how much more to you, both in the flesh and in the Lord."],[2142.97,"Well, what's that saying?"],[2145.21,"Not just you need to forgive him and take him back into your household as a slave, but he's like a brother to me."],[2156.17,"And how much more is he a brother to you?"],[2160.41,"You can't enslave your own brother."],[2164.38,"No one would do that."],[2170.7,"Verse 17. If then you count me as a partner, receive him as you would mean"],[2179.04,"So if you if I'm your friend."],[2182.34,"Then receive him the way you'd receive me."],[2184.34,"Would he receive St."],[2185.38,"Paul as a slave?"],[2187.14,"No. Verse 18, but if he has wronged you or owes anything, put that on my account."],[2202.34,"I, Paul, am writing with my own hand."],[2204.98,"This is one of those times where he writes with his own hand."],[2208.1,"I will repay."],[2211.38,"Not to mention to you that you owe me even your own self besides."],[2218.06,"I'll pay it."],[2219.02,"Look, I'm I'm writing my name here."],[2221.26,"This is an IOU."],[2222.38,"I will pay whatever he owes you."],[2224.78,"I'm not even going to bring up how much you owe me."],[2227.18,"But you know, anyway, moving on."],[2229.34,"Moving on. Verse 20 Yes, brother, let me have joy from you in the Lord."],[2238.3,"Refresh my heart in the Lord."],[2243.18,"Having confidence in your obedience, I write to you, knowing that you will do even more than I say."],[2251.68,"I just know, knowing you as I do, that you're gonna go even above and beyond what I'm asking you."],[2260.49,"Verse 22, but meanwhile, also prepare a guest room for me, for I trust that through your prayers I shall be granted to you."],[2268.65,"You know what?"],[2269.37,"As soon as I'm out of this prison, I'm gonna come visit you at your house."],[2275.05,"Just a friendly visit."],[2277.45,"Not to see what happened."],[2281.97,"Verse 23, Epaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, greets you, as do Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, Luke, my fellow laborers."],[2290.45,"The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit."],[2293.49,"Amen. I would not St."],[2298.93,"Paul could sell well to used car in this time."],[2302.33,"Paul is basically told obedience to free him"],[2303.46,"So St. Yeah."],[2310.82,"Without telling obedience to free him."],[2314.95,"So that in and of itself should tell us something about how St."],[2317.91,"Paul viewed slavery."],[2319.27,"But again, he wasn't viewing it from this idea that morality is having the right opinion about institutions or abstractions."],[2327.29,"He's talking, he's looking at these two particular people."],[2331.29,"He's saying, what's the right thing for these two people to do?"],[2336.99,"And it's right for Saint Onesimus to go back."],[2342.83,"It's not right for him to be a runaway fugitive."],[2345.07,"It's right for him to go back"],[2347.8,"But it's right for Saint Philemon to free him and receive him as a brother."],[2352.92,"Keep him as part of the household, but not as an enslaved person anymore."],[2357.56,"That's the right thing for these two people to do."],[2360.2,"And that's what he's about bringing about."],[2364.6,"And while it is a little used car salesman ish."],[2368.89,"What is St."],[2369.77,"Paul doing here, though?"],[2373.45,"What is he doing?"],[2374.49,"He's doing everything possible to make it easy and beneficial for St."],[2381.85,"Philemon to do the right thing."],[2386.81,"Having this letter out there. makes it very easy for him to receive back St"],[2393.49,"Onesimus to forgive him and to free him."],[2398.53,"And keep him in his household."],[2399.89,"Hard for him not to, but easy for him to do."],[2402.37,"And when he does it, he'll be applauded, he'll be credited, people will be thanking the Lord for him."],[2411.22,"So St. Paul says, this is the right thing for him to do."],[2415.06,"And I'm going to, as much as is in my power, being a couple cities away and imprisoned, to make it really easy for him to do the right thing."],[2426.42,"Very hard to do the wrong thing."],[2429.11,"I'm going to open all the doors, he just has to walk through."],[2433.25,"For the right thing to happen here."],[2437.5,"And sometimes that approach is more effective than just telling people not to do the wrong thing."],[2447.66,"Or berating them about doing the wrong thing."],[2454.22,"Well, we we know it was effective, but I think it was far more effective for St."],[2459.5,"Paul to do this than for St."],[2462.06,"Paul to send him an angry note saying slavery is evil."],[2466.38,"You must free all your slaves."],[2468.54,"I don't care what he did."],[2470.54,"How dare you, you evil, you know oppressor."],[2476.71,"That w that that may, again, that may be what we think is morally right opinion."],[2483.21,"But that would not functionally have produced the result, the good result that St."],[2490.33,"Paul wanted. He doesn't lie in here."],[2494.81,"He doesn't say anything that isn't true."],[2498.11,"Everything he says is true."],[2499.31,"Everything he says about how he feels is true."],[2503.17,"But he says it in such a way and he sets it up to make it really easy to do the right thing and really hard to do the wrong thing."],[2511.09,"You catch more flies with honey than you do with vinegar."],[2514.73,"This is exhibit A of what St."],[2517.29,"Paul said, he's all things to all men, that by all means he might save some."],[2524.55,"If he needs to turn on the charm to get the right result, he'll turn on the charm."],[2528.71,"If he needs to be stern with something, we've seen him rebuke some people pretty sternly in his epistles."],[2533.45,"If he needs to do that, if that's what's going to get the result, then that's what he'll do."],[2538.11,"If you need to, if to bring somebody to repentance, you have to excommunicate him from the church and turn them over to Satan to bring them to repentance and salvation, then that's what you have to do."],[2546.91,"If you've got to butter them up, tell them lots of nice things they want to hear to get them to do the right thing, then that's what you need to do."],[2554.45,"And that's not being fake or phony."],[2558.13,"That that's approaching the situation the way it needs to be approached. to get the right result"],[2565.02,"Christ himself talked to tax collectors and repentant prostitutes differently than he talked to the Pharisees."],[2572.22,"But he had the same goal both times."],[2574.7,"He wanted the Pharisees to repent and come to salvation, and he wanted the tax collectors and the prostitutes to come to repentance and salvation."],[2583.58,"But he approached it in different ways to get there."],[2589.34,"Yes. Yeah. And so beyond just the slavery issue and beyond just uh uh St."],[2598.3,"Paul's tone, it's good that we have this little short paragraph."],[2603.0,"This little short epistle here at the end of the collection added in because it shows us another piece of of who Saint Paul is, how he did what he did, and part of another part of the apostolic approach to to life in Christ that we wouldn't necessarily see other places."],[2631.6,"Yeah. And I think that's why."],[2634.0,"Uh-huh. It gives us another piece of of that sort of public discourse of the apostles and how they approached these things."],[2645.9,"So um, yeah, this is this is the only right."],[2651.06,"The other epistles of St."],[2652.42,"Paul we have that were written to a single person were written to people who he was mentoring."],[2658.28,"St. Timothy and St."],[2659.48,"Titus. St. Philemon is a member of one of his churches, a prominent member, but a member of one of his churches."],[2667.4,"So this shows us how St."],[2668.76,"Paul talked to someone like that about an issue."],[2671.8,"So Philemon is not mentioned as a companion of Paul or anything like that."],[2676.36,"No. No, but he did later become a bishop, as did St."],[2680.04,"Onesimus. In that same region."],[2685.08,"Yes. Ironically. Later on."],[2691.0,"Yeah, after after being freed."],[2694.64,"But there were, even in even in the the patristic period, there were literally deacons and priests and bishops who were enslaved people."],[2706.88,"Who in society had one role and in Christ's church had a very different role."],[2715.6,"Even bishops. Yeah."],[2720.96,"So, yeah. Because the way authority works in in the church and the way authority works in the world are two different things."],[2730.4,"Yeah. Okay, so that's it for these two short epistles."],[2735.92,"And next week we'll have the probably epically long introduction to St."],[2742.6,"Paul's epistle to the Hebrews."],[2745.72,"Um I'll just call it that."],[2749.16,"So thank you, everybody."],[2752.44,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2759.8,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2765.16,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.64,"lv":2,"h":"Why Philemon is last","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even shorter epistle than Titus</li><li class=\"d0\">Written <b>much earlier</b> than the pastorals</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's epistles ordered <b>longest to shortest</b>, not chronologically</li><li class=\"d1\">exceptions: <b>1-2 Corinthians</b>, <b>1-2 Thessalonians</b>, <b>1-2 Timothy</b> kept together</li><li class=\"d1\">Philemon last only because it is the <b>shortest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews: earliest copy is in a Pauline collection <b>right after Romans</b> - because of length</li><li class=\"d1\">moved in modern English NTs on belief Paul <b>didn't write it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more next week</li><li class=\"d0\">We take <b>book order</b> for granted - it isn't original</li><li class=\"d1\">4th c. Greek NTs often had <b>no Revelation</b>; Gospels, general epistles (James, Peter, John), <i>then</i> Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">OT order \"all over the place\": Hebrew Bible vs Greek OT vs <b>Slavonic</b> vs Protestant</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Anyone making a <b>theological argument from book order</b> - ignore everything they say\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sure sign they don't know the Bible's history</li></ul>"},{"t":215.68,"lv":2,"h":"Date and public-letter nature","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Written same time as <b>Ephesians</b> and <b>Colossians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>in prison in Ephesus</b> - he was in prison a lot</li><li class=\"d1\">c. <b>50-51 AD</b> vs mid-60s for the pastorals</li><li class=\"d0\">Shared with pastorals: written to a <b>single person</b>, not a community</li><li class=\"d1\">but as a <b>public letter</b> - particular person, particular issue</li><li class=\"d0\">Illustration: letter to \"my good friend <b>Arthur</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not gonna come down hard... but rethink XYZ\" - then <b>50 copies to the whole church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how it's put out <b>belies the tone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">far more <b>onus to follow through</b> with everyone looking</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep in mind reading: Paul <b>soft and deferent</b> yet <b>speaking with authority</b></li></ul>"},{"t":356.72,"lv":2,"h":"The issue: Onesimus the runaway","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Onesimus</b> = an <b>enslaved person</b>; <b>St. Philemon</b> = his master</li><li class=\"d0\">Both <b>Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some issue made Onesimus <b>flee</b> - \"one of the first rules\" you can't do</li><li class=\"d0\">Came to Paul to <b>intercede</b> with Philemon</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>common in ancient world</b>: disobedient, misunderstanding, stole, or ran off</li><li class=\"d1\">way back into good graces = go to someone of <b>higher social status</b> than the master</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel: a letter of <b>Pliny</b> doing exactly what Paul does</li><li class=\"d1\">letter on behalf of enslaved person to his master</li><li class=\"d1\">Pliny adopts a somewhat different tone</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: both Christians, Paul an <b>apostle</b> = higher status</li></ul>"},{"t":512.7,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's view of slavery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul does <b>not</b> consider slavery a good thing</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Timothy 1:9-10</b> list of bad folks</li><li class=\"d1\">unholy, profane, murderers of fathers/mothers, manslayers, fornicators, sodomites</li><li class=\"d1\">NKJV \"kidnappers\" = Greek <i>andrapodistes</i> = literally <b>\"Caesars of men\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>slave traders</b> - those who enslave and sell</li><li class=\"d1\">listed alongside <b>parent-murderers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But must talk about <b>Roman slavery</b> - continuities and discontinuities with what modern Americans picture</li></ul>"},{"t":594.63,"lv":3,"h":"Continuities with North Atlantic slavery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We picture <b>North Atlantic slave trade</b>, chattel slavery</li><li class=\"d1\">Africans, but also <b>natives</b> more than we're aware</li><li class=\"d0\">Points of continuity</li><li class=\"d1\">humans <b>owned</b> by another human</li><li class=\"d1\">considered <b>subhuman</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no racial element in Roman slavery</li><li class=\"d1\">nor really at the start of the Atlantic trade - <b>racism grew out of slavery</b> to justify it</li><li class=\"d1\">exploited in every way - labor \"literally work to death\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sexual exploitation</b> common in both</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul told enslaved people being <b>raped</b> they were <b>not sinning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"no matter what their body did\" - not immoral, not wicked, not participating</li><li class=\"d1\">extended the <b>hope of the gospel</b> and God's healing to them</li></ul>"},{"t":762.81,"lv":3,"h":"The fathers against slavery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Roman slavery didn't go away - still around in <b>Chrysostom's</b> time</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Gregory of Nyssa</b> preached against it - <b>two Paschal homilies in a row</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you can't own fellow Christians - <b>free them</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">incompatible with the gospel and what humanity is in Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chrysostom</b> rails on and on about it</li><li class=\"d1\">even his Christian congregants were <b>sexually exploiting</b> enslaved people in their households</li><li class=\"d0\">Exploitative, esp. sexual, element attacked directly by Christianity</li></ul>"},{"t":853.48,"lv":3,"h":"Discontinuities: function and structure","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Roman slavery had different <b>function and structure</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some enslaved by <b>war</b> - lost a battle, annexed</li><li class=\"d0\">Many by <b>debt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no bankruptcy system - <b>work off the debt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like collection agencies - debt (and you) could be <b>sold</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Enslaved people part of Roman <b>households</b> under the <i>pater familias</i></li><li class=\"d1\">true earlier too: <b>Torah</b> - freed after set time, all freed in <b>Jubilee</b> (if followed - Israel didn't)</li><li class=\"d1\">may <b>choose to stay</b> in household</li><li class=\"d1\">ritual at household entrance - brought before <b>ancestors</b>, <b>permanently family</b> (more at Exodus)</li></ul>"},{"t":985.0,"lv":3,"h":"Household roles, not always exploitative","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No public schools - wealthy families had an enslaved <b>tutor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">might be well treated</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>Alfred</b> to Bruce Wayne - technically servant, really family, \"almost surrogate dad\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So not always completely exploitative - Paul <b>allows for that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Suddenly freed and tossed out? <b>No job market</b> - \"pre-capitalism by a lot\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no trade to earn a living</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>guilds of freedmen</b> - learn trades, support each other</li><li class=\"d1\">like people leaving prison today</li><li class=\"d0\">Reasons not to just <b>do away with the institution</b> until society restructured</li></ul>"},{"t":1112.54,"lv":3,"h":"Why no instant abolition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul and most fathers don't say \"free everybody\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Gregory is the exception</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chrysostom</b>: strict rules for treating household slaves</li><li class=\"d1\">respect and dignity for them and their <b>bodies</b>, Christian or not</li><li class=\"d1\">but not \"free them no matter what, wanted or not\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some modern readers: nothing acceptable but \"<b>evil, end now</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Rome wasn't built in a day</b>\" - can't restructure all social institutions in a day</li><li class=\"d0\">Some must go <b>immediately</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pederasty</b> - \"zero value, it's done\"</li><li class=\"d1\">certain elements of slavery</li><li class=\"d1\">others <b>worked out over time</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1210.18,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: craftsmen and generational slavery","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>craftsmen</b> and shopkeepers who were slaves?</li><li class=\"d1\">yes - estate wants a <b>blacksmith</b>, then need ends</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>loan them out</b> to the public, take proceeds - or sell</li><li class=\"d0\">Late Republic: enslaving thousands of <b>Greeks</b>, some trained craftsmen</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Generational</b> slaves: past that, maybe taught by father</li><li class=\"d1\">many just <b>field work</b> all life - agricultural economy</li><li class=\"d1\">freed tomorrow: no land - \"enslave yourself to somebody else\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>military</b> service gave land at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">OT: <b>Jacob</b> works <b>7 years</b> for Laban for Rachel - labor as dowry, then \"wrong wife\" - 7 more</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>indentured servitude</b>; lines were thin</li><li class=\"d1\">enslavement usually <b>whole families</b>; indenture an individual for a set period</li></ul>"},{"t":1418.69,"lv":2,"h":"Morality as opinion vs. deeds","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Puritan thinking at base of American thought</li><li class=\"d1\">morality = <b>holding the correct opinion</b> about what's wrong</li><li class=\"d1\">what matters is what we <b>believe</b>, not do - very Protestant</li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul or Chrysostom not saying \"slavery is evil, abolish it\" = <b>immoral</b></li><li class=\"d0\">True across the political spectrum</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>abortion</b>: all agree it's wrong; haven't helped one unwed mother, \"haven't spent a penny\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what makes you bad = not agreeing it's bad</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>woke</b>: racism and poverty bad, \"we don't like poor people much\" - agreeing makes us good</li><li class=\"d0\">Not how Paul or fathers approached morality</li></ul>"},{"t":1566.26,"lv":3,"h":"Paul cares about fruit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Titus</b>: didn't care what false teachers thought good or bad</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>look at their deeds</b>\" - evil</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharisees</b> held right opinions - Christ called them <b>hypocrites</b> for how they lived</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul cares how enslaved people are <b>treated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how they live as Christians while enslaved</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>them and masters</b> finding salvation</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Bad news for <b>hashtag activism</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">tweeting an opinion is not moral or immoral - <b>what we do</b> is</li><li class=\"d1\">goats: \"you saw me in prison and <b>thought that was bad</b>\"? No - ignored or <b>visited</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1683.37,"lv":2,"h":"Philemon 1-3: the greeting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads vv. 4-7 first by mistake - restarts at the top</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 1</b>: \"Paul, a <b>prisoner of Christ Jesus</b>, and Timothy our brother\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy as co-author like <b>Ephesians</b>, <b>Colossians</b> - one way we date it</li><li class=\"d0\">To <b>Philemon</b>, \"beloved friend and fellow laborer\"</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>Apphia</b> (maybe wife), <b>Archippus</b> (son or household member), \"church in your house\"</li><li class=\"d1\">household = focal point and meeting place of local Christians</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 3</b>: \"Grace to you and peace\" - usual introduction</li></ul>"},{"t":1823.99,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: where was Philemon","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: do you know where he is? - Paul doesn't say</li><li class=\"d0\">Both Philemon and Onesimus <b>later bishops</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Philemon <b>bishop of Colossae</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Onesimus <b>bishop in Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same region as Paul's letters to <b>Ephesians</b> and <b>Colossians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Onesimus could carry the letter back from Paul imprisoned at Ephesus</li></ul>"},{"t":1867.39,"lv":2,"h":"Philemon 4-7: thanksgiving","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I thank my God... hearing of your <b>love and faith</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same kind of things he says in <b>positive letters to communities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I give thanks for the good things you're doing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not lying - Philemon clearly <b>faithful, important</b> in community (later bishop)</li><li class=\"d0\">v. 7: \"<b>brother</b>\" - hearts of the saints refreshed by you</li><li class=\"d1\">brother not \"son\" - <b>coming alongside, arm around him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"my good friend, my beloved friend, my brother\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1935.95,"lv":2,"h":"Philemon 8-11: appeal for Onesimus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>We need to talk for a second</b>\" - note the <b>therefore</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 8-9</b>: bold enough in Christ to <b>command</b>, yet <b>for love's sake appeal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm an apostle, could just tell you\" - but just <b>ask</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Paul the aged, now prisoner\" - \"just little old Paul over here in prison\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 10-11</b>: \"my son <b>Onesimus</b>, begotten in my chains\"</li><li class=\"d1\">once <b>unprofitable</b>, now <b>profitable</b> to you and me</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you thought he was worthless\" - now <b>spiritual son</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2031.53,"lv":2,"h":"Philemon 12-16: no longer a slave","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 12-14</b>: sending him back - \"my own <b>heart</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wanted to keep him to serve <b>on your behalf</b>, giving you the credit</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing <b>without your consent</b> - good deed <b>voluntary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul publishing this <b>publicly</b> - chance to be congratulated for the good in the introduction</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 15</b>: \"perhaps he departed for a while... that you might receive him <b>forever</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe why he left in the first place - to come to Paul and grow</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 16</b>: \"no longer a slave, but more than a slave, a <b>beloved brother</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you can't <b>enslave your own brother</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2170.7,"lv":2,"h":"Philemon 17-25: IOU and close","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 17</b>: \"if you count me a <b>partner</b>, receive him as you would me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">would he receive Paul as a slave? No</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 18-19</b>: put it on my account - \"I, Paul, write with my <b>own hand</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">an <b>IOU</b>; \"not to mention you owe me even your own self\" - \"moving on\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 20-21</b>: \"refresh my heart\"; confident you'll do <b>even more than I say</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 22</b>: \"prepare a <b>guest room</b>\" - coming after release</li><li class=\"d1\">\"just a friendly visit, not to see what happened\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 23-25</b>: Epaphras, <b>Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, Luke</b> greet you</li><li class=\"d1\">grace of our Lord be with your spirit</li></ul>"},{"t":2293.49,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's method: open every door","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Almost a \"used car salesman\" - never tells Philemon outright to free him</li><li class=\"d1\">that itself shows Paul's view of slavery</li><li class=\"d0\">Not morality as opinion about institutions - looks at <b>two particular people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Onesimus: right to <b>go back</b>, not be a fugitive</li><li class=\"d1\">Philemon: right to <b>free him and receive as brother</b>, keep in household</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul makes it <b>easy and beneficial</b> to do right</li><li class=\"d1\">letter out there: hard not to, easy to - he'll be <b>applauded</b>, credited</li><li class=\"d1\">\"open all the doors, he just has to walk through\"</li><li class=\"d0\">More effective than <b>telling people not to do wrong</b> or berating</li><li class=\"d1\">vs angry note: \"slavery is evil, free all your slaves, how dare you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">may be our \"right opinion\" - wouldn't produce the <b>good result</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2490.33,"lv":3,"h":"Honest, but all things to all men","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">He doesn't lie - everything he says is <b>true</b>, including feelings</li><li class=\"d0\">Sets it up: <b>easy to do right, hard to do wrong</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"more flies with honey than vinegar\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Exhibit A</b>: \"all things to all men, that by all means I might save some\"</li><li class=\"d1\">turn on the <b>charm</b> if needed</li><li class=\"d1\">be <b>stern</b> if needed - rebuked some pretty sternly</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>excommunicate</b>, hand over to Satan for repentance</li><li class=\"d1\">butter them up with nice things - if that gets the result</li><li class=\"d0\">Not fake or phony - <b>approaching it as it needs approaching</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2565.02,"lv":3,"h":"Christ: same goal, different approach","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christ spoke to <b>tax collectors and repentant prostitutes</b> differently than to <b>Pharisees</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same goal: <b>repentance and salvation</b> for both</li><li class=\"d1\">different ways to get there</li></ul>"},{"t":2589.34,"lv":2,"h":"Why Philemon is in the canon","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beyond slavery and Paul's tone - shows <b>who Paul is</b> and how he worked</li><li class=\"d0\">Another piece of the <b>apostolic approach to life in Christ</b>, public discourse of apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Only other single-person epistles: <b>Timothy</b> and <b>Titus</b> - men he was <b>mentoring</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Philemon = a <b>prominent member of one of his churches</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shows how Paul talked to someone like that about an issue</li><li class=\"d0\">Not mentioned as a companion of Paul</li></ul>"},{"t":2676.36,"lv":3,"h":"Enslaved clergy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Philemon and Onesimus both later <b>bishops</b> in that region</li><li class=\"d1\">Onesimus - \"ironically\" - after being freed</li><li class=\"d0\">Even in patristic period: <b>deacons, priests, bishops who were enslaved</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one role in society, a very different role in Christ's church</li><li class=\"d0\">Authority in the Church and in the world = <b>two different things</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Hebrews</b> - the \"probably epically long\" <b>introduction</b>"});
